  "markdown_report": "The best next step at 10% progress is an **anchor-preserving HSTU context-access audit**. It tests whether recent-history sensitivity survives control of positional geometry before motivating training changes. No measured improvement is available yet.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Current deletion probes change evidence and positions together. Their effects cannot isolate content dependence. (2) Removing the latest item also changes the readout anchor. This requires a separate condition from context interventions. (3) Training-target probes measure fitted-model sensitivity. Their bootstrap intervals do not establish generalization.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) HSTU separates content interaction, relative bias and multiplicative gating [1]. These provide distinct intervention points. (2) Recency and compatibility research motivates examining specific recent connections [2,3]. Neither history similarity nor co-purchase establishes purchase purpose or device compatibility. (3) Sampled ranking can disagree with full-catalog ranking [4]. Therefore, auxiliary diagnostics cannot replace the pinned host metric.\n\n**Framework and directions.** Organize evidence along three axes: intervention mechanism (context access, relative bias, anchor change), evidence source (training sensitivity, host validation, exploratory feedback), and claim strength (observed response, proxy association, unsupported intent). This separates architectural sensitivity, temporal relevance and evaluation reliability while exposing the missing controlled comparison.\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality/potential/code difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means more implementation effort.\n\n- **Matched context-access audit [1]: 6/8/5.** Originality: controlled HSTU adaptation; established ablation principles. Potential: identifies actionable mechanisms; sensitivity may not predict gains. Difficulty: narrow intervention interface; attention internals require inspection.\n- **Anchor-preserving context dropout [1,2]: 4/7/4.** Originality: targeted regularization; dropout is familiar. Potential: may reduce brittle context reliance; can erase useful recent evidence. Difficulty: modest training change; exact masking must match inference.\n- **Evidence-qualified recommendation audit [3]: 5/6/3.** Originality: explicit unknown states; metadata audits already exist. Potential: clarifies unsupported compatibility claims; sparse descriptions limit coverage. Difficulty: lightweight text rules; ambiguous attributes require review.\n\n**Selected algorithm.** Freeze each category’s single HSTU. For eligible training users, keep the current target and 99 unique unseen negatives fixed. Match recent and older context sets by size, excluding the final observed item. Block selected key positions at every HSTU layer while preserving slots, timestamps, query validity and the original readout index. Compare intact, recent-blocked and older-blocked scores; separately ablate available position/time biases.\n\nReport paired HR@10, target-rank and margin changes, user-bootstrap intervals, exclusions and missingness. These remain in-sample sensitivity estimates. Preserve the diagnostic interfaces, four-category routing and one checkpoint per category. Implement hooks against the actual SiLU attention; ordinary softmax masking may be invalid. Any subsequent training experiment retains seeds 0/42, the supplied splits, 200 maximum epochs, 200 batches per epoch and host macro validation HR@10 selection.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Audit each category’s single trained HSTU with matched recent-versus-older attention-access interventions that preserve sequence slots and the final observed readout item. Separate context access effects from relative position/time bias and anchor removal.",
    "motivation": "Existing compacted deletion probes confound removed evidence with positional changes. Qualitative feedback suggests insufficient prioritization of recent specific connections, but supplies no measured evidence that recency weighting will improve validation HR@10. A controlled sensitivity audit is a feasible first step before spending the shared training budget.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), including metrics and metric_definitions. Preserve log2feats(sequence), item_emb and predict_score(features, items); optional intervention hooks must not change default forward behavior. Determine the readout using the greatest nonzero sequence index, never count-1. Inspect the supplied HSTU attention implementation before adding hooks: block selected key contributions in every layer without changing its ordinary causal mask, normalization or sequence-length scaling. Apply blocking after any transformation that could reintroduce a nonzero contribution. Preserve timestamps and slots, and ablate position/time biases separately only when implemented. Run frozen probes in evaluation mode under no_grad, restoring RNG and training state. Bootstrap users within categories; average category effects equally only when all four reports are available. Report eligible counts and uncertainty. Use training histories only for this in-sample audit; do not describe the target as untouched. Treat human feedback from twenty users as exploratory and compatibility as unknown without explicit metadata evidence. Preserve DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'] and trial-local artifacts. Any later architecture change must update the embedded predictor writer and load exactly one trained checkpoint per category. No executable baseline replacement is specified because the actual backbone source was not supplied.",
    "pseudocode": "for category in four_categories:\n    freeze(category_model)\n    for history in eligible_training_histories:\n        target = history[-1]\n        sequence = left_pad(history[:-1])\n        anchor = greatest_nonzero_index(sequence)\n        recent, older = matched_context_positions(sequence, exclude=anchor)\n        candidates = fixed_target_and_99_unique_unseen_negatives(history)\n        intact = score(sequence, candidates, readout=anchor)\n        for selected in [recent, older]:\n            scores = score_with_blocked_keys_at_every_layer(\n                sequence, candidates, selected, readout=anchor,\n                preserve_slots=True, preserve_timestamps=True)\n            record_paired_rank_hit_and_margin_changes(intact, scores)\n        separately_probe_supported_relative_bias_components()\n    summarize_with_user_bootstrap_and_explicit_definitions()\n# Use unchanged host macro validation HR@10 to select subsequent trained variants.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "Combines matched context interventions, fixed readout geometry and HSTU-specific component separation into a focused diagnostic design.",
      "negative": "The underlying methods are established ablation and paired-evaluation techniques; this is a proposed adaptation, not a demonstrated new learning algorithm."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 8,
      "positive": "Could distinguish useful targets for context dropout, relative-bias regularization and masking corrections within one HSTU.",
      "negative": "Frozen-model sensitivity may fail to predict which training changes improve the benchmark."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Reuses existing users, candidate panels, scoring interfaces and bootstrap reporting without fitting additional models.",
      "negative": "Correct intervention hooks require access to actual attention internals and careful treatment of SiLU masking and indirect paths across layers."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
